### A robot is a chain of rigid bodies {#rigid}

Quadrupeds, drones, dexterous hands and arms look like different problems, but almost all of them can be described the same way: as rigid bodies moving through space [▶ 02:09](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=129). "Rigid" means the distance between any two points on the body never changes, so one frame attached to the body tells you where all of it is [▶ 02:39](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=159). On the ground, a pose takes three numbers (x, y and a heading), the group called SE(2); in space it takes six (three positions, plus roll, pitch and yaw), SE(3). In code a pose is a matrix that holds a rotation and a translation [▶ 03:41](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=221), and a rotation matrix must be orthogonal with determinant 1, so it never stretches or mirrors the body [▶ 04:44](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=284).

The payoff is chaining. A self-driving car's lidar sees a pedestrian in *its own* frame; to decide whether to brake, the car needs the pedestrian in its *base* frame. Multiplying the matrices that relate the frames does the conversion [▶ 05:14](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=314). [Worked example 1](#ex-frames) does it with numbers, and @eq-homogeneous gives the formula.

### Joints, degrees of freedom and three spaces {#spaces}

An arm or a humanoid is not one rigid body but several: **links** connected by **joints**, ending in an **end effector** such as a gripper [▶ 07:19](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=439). The end effector shapes the action space: a suction gripper is on or off, a parallel gripper has one opening width, a dexterous hand can have more than 20 degrees of freedom [▶ 09:24](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=564). Most robot joints are **revolute**: they rotate about one axis, like an elbow [▶ 10:27](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=627).

The **degrees of freedom** count how many independent numbers fix the robot's configuration [▶ 11:31](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=691); the Grübler–Kutzbach formula (@eq-grubler) counts them from the links and joints [▶ 12:04](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=724). Three spaces then matter:

- The **configuration space** is the set of all joint configurations [▶ 12:35](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=755). For an arm with two revolute joints it is not a flat square but a torus, because each angle wraps around: 359° and 1° are 2° apart, not 358° [▶ 13:05](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=785). A network that takes joint angles as input has to respect that.
- The **workspace** is the set of points the hand can reach [▶ 13:40](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=820). The robot perceives the task here (the cup is on the table) but acts in configuration space (turn these joints).
- The **task space** is where the objective lives, independent of the robot: for wiping a whiteboard, the board's surface [▶ 16:45](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=1005). A 7-joint arm placing its hand in a 6-number pose has one joint to spare. That **redundancy** lets it swing its elbow while holding a mug perfectly still, which helps it avoid obstacles [▶ 18:54](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=1134).

### An obstacle becomes a shape in joint space {#c-obstacle}

Put a round obstacle next to a two-joint arm. In the workspace it is a disc. In configuration space it is the set of all (shoulder, elbow) angle pairs at which some part of the arm touches the disc, and that set has a very different shape [▶ 14:43](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=883). In the toy below, a disc of radius 0\.25 blocks 8\.2% of all joint configurations, all at shoulder angles between \-18° and 94°. Between 32° and 46° the upper arm itself reaches the disc, so every elbow angle is blocked: a bar across the whole square. More than 6° away from the bar on either side, only the forearm can hit, and only when it is bent towards the disc: with a positive elbow angle below the bar, a negative one above it.

The 1990s recipe for motion planning was to build exactly this picture, mark the forbidden region, and search the rest for a path [▶ 15:14](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=914). Moving both joints at a steady rate from start to goal, a straight line in configuration space, sweeps the arm through the disc: 20% of that line is blocked. A breadth-first search over the free cells finds a detour of 116 grid moves, against 65 for the straight line: it turns the shoulder the other way, through 180°, and swings the arm around below the base. The recipe stops scaling as the number of joints grows: the grid has $180^n$ cells for $n$ joints. Learned policies instead pick up, from experience, which actions lead into trouble, without building the map [▶ 15:45](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=945).


