### Forward is easy, inverse is a search {#kinematics}

**Forward kinematics** answers: given all joint angles, where is the hand? Multiply the transforms along the chain and read off the answer; there is exactly one [▶ 19:24](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=1164). **Inverse kinematics** asks the reverse, which is the question a policy usually poses: "put the hand *there*; which joint angles do that?" [▶ 19:55](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=1195). The answer can be two configurations (elbow bent one way or the other), infinitely many (a redundant arm), or none (out of reach). For complex robots there is often no closed-form solution, so IK is solved as an optimization: measure the distance between the hand and the target, and nudge the joints downhill [▶ 20:58](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=1258). The nudge uses the **Jacobian**, which says how much the hand moves when each joint turns a little [▶ 21:30](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=1290). Which solution the descent finds depends on where it starts (@fig-ik).

### Smooth moves and a controller that keeps correcting {#control}

Knowing the goal angles is not enough: the robot cannot teleport. The simplest way to get there is **linear interpolation (LERP)**: split the move into evenly spaced waypoints [▶ 23:03](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=1383). The trouble is at the ends. The joint jumps from rest to full speed in one step and stops just as abruptly, which demands huge accelerations and shakes the hardware [▶ 24:07](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=1447). **Quintic time scaling** replaces the straight line with a fifth-degree polynomial whose velocity and acceleration are zero at both ends [▶ 24:38](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=1478) (@eq-quintic, @fig-profiles).

Even perfect waypoints are not followed perfectly: gravity, friction and imperfect motors push the joint off course. A **PID controller** corrects continuously [▶ 26:10](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=1570). The lecture's picture: the proportional term is a spring that pulls harder the further off you are; the integral term is "anti-gravity", building up to cancel a steady load; the derivative term is a damper that stops overshoot [▶ 27:12](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=1632). In this page's toy, a single joint lifts a rod against gravity. With only the spring, it swings back and forth through the whole move and the hold after it, over a range of 21° in the last half second, and the swings are still growing. Adding the damper stops the swinging but leaves the rod hanging 2\.5° low, and the integral term removes that sag (@fig-pid). With LERP waypoints instead of quintic ones, the controller's largest change of torque between two physics steps is 6\.6 times larger.

### From control to decisions {#decisions}

Control makes a robot follow a path. Real tasks need more: a sequence of decisions toward a long-term goal in a world that is noisy and only partly known [▶ 28:14](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=1694). The lecture's example is a two-armed robot cutting (toy) sushi, a precise, long task with 14 degrees of freedom to command many times a second, while low-level controllers make the motors follow [▶ 28:45](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=1725).

The decision rule is the **policy**. It looks like an image classifier: input in, scores over outputs out. A camera image of a snowy road goes in; probabilities for "left", "straight", "right" come out [▶ 30:51](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=1851). The difference is consequential: a classifier that mistakes a cat for a dog leaves the cat unchanged, but a policy that turns left into a ditch changes everything it will see next [▶ 32:23](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=1943). In general a policy outputs a distribution (a **stochastic** policy); a **deterministic** one puts all its probability on one action [▶ 32:55](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=1975).

What the policy sees is an **observation**, not the **state**. The state is the full truth (the car's exact position, speed, heading); the observation is what the camera makes of it, which changes with snow or darkness while the state does not [▶ 33:25](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=2005). The state has the **Markov property**: once you know it, the past adds nothing to predicting the future [▶ 34:59](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=2099). Observations usually do not.

### The MDP: one language for every robot {#mdp-intuition}

A **Markov decision process** packages this into four parts: the states, the actions, the transition probabilities, and a reward function [▶ 37:03](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=2223). Its value is generality: the same learning algorithms then apply to a humanoid with 27 degrees of freedom and to chess [▶ 37:34](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=2254).

- **States and actions** can be discrete (squares on a chessboard, arrow keys) or continuous (joint angles, a VR controller's motion) [▶ 38:40](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=2320). In a continuous space you cannot keep a table entry for every state, so you need function approximators such as neural networks.
- **Transitions** are deterministic only in an ideal world. Turn the steering wheel 5° on ice and the car may not follow the arc you expect, so robotics models the next state as a random draw [▶ 39:11](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=2351).
- **Rewards** can be **sparse**, such as 1 for lifting the object and 0 otherwise, or **dense**, such as minus the hand's distance to the object. The lecturer's pick-and-place agent failed with the sparse reward and learned with the dense one [▶ 40:45](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=2445). This page's random-search toy shows why: with a sparse reward, almost every trial scores 0, and comparing zeros teaches nothing (@fig-random-search).

A run of the policy is a **trajectory**: state, action, state, action, and so on. Its probability multiplies the chance of the start state, the policy's choice at each step, and the world's response [▶ 42:20](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=2540). Tasks have a **horizon**: a fixed number of steps, or until the episode ends [▶ 42:50](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=2570). Over an endless horizon, rewards are **discounted** by a factor $\gamma$ per step so the total stays finite and nearer rewards count more [▶ 44:23](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=2663). The goal is then the policy with the highest expected discounted return [▶ 44:54](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=2694).

The discount is not a technicality: it changes which behavior is best. In the corridor toy below, a robot can walk 2 cells left to a reward of 1 or 6 cells right to a reward of 10. Going right is better only when $\gamma$ is above 0\.562. If each move slips the wrong way with probability 0.2, the far reward takes longer to reach on average, and the threshold rises to 0\.643 (@eq-flip).

## Key concepts & formulae

The lecture writes $s_t$ for the state, $o_t$ for the observation and $a_t$ for the action at step $t$, following dynamic programming; optimal control writes $x_t$ and $u_t$, and a cost $c(x, u) = -r(s, a)$ [▶ 36:03](https://youtu.be/5-Bb84eTTqQ?t=2163). The ADM book writes the transition model as $T(s' \mid s, a)$ where the lecture writes $P(s_{t+1} \mid s_t, a_t)$, and the value of a policy as $U^\pi$.

### Poses and frames

::: {.key-equation data-name="Homogeneous transform in the plane, SE(2)"}
$$
\begin{gathered}
T = \begin{bmatrix} \cos\theta & -\sin\theta & t_x \\ \sin\theta & \cos\theta & t_y \\ 0 & 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix} \\[4pt]
\begin{bmatrix} \mathbf{p}^{\text{base}} \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} = T_{\text{base}\leftarrow\text{sensor}} \begin{bmatrix} \mathbf{p}^{\text{sensor}} \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
\end{gathered}
$$ {#eq-homogeneous}
:::

**In words.** One matrix stores a rotation by $\theta$ and a shift by $(t_x, t_y)$. Multiplying a point, written with an extra 1, by the matrix that relates two frames gives the same point's coordinates in the other frame.

**Term by term.**

- The top-left $2 \times 2$ block is the rotation $R$; the last column is the translation $\mathbf{t}$.
- The extra 1 lets one matrix product apply both the rotation and the shift.
- $T_{\text{base}\leftarrow\text{sensor}}$: the sensor's pose in the base frame. Chains compose by multiplication: $T_{\text{world}\leftarrow\text{sensor}} = T_{\text{world}\leftarrow\text{base}}\, T_{\text{base}\leftarrow\text{sensor}}$.
- In 3D, SE(3) uses a $4 \times 4$ matrix with a $3 \times 3$ rotation, 6 degrees of freedom in all.
- A valid rotation satisfies $R^\top R = I$ and $\det R = 1$: it keeps lengths and does not mirror.

### Degrees of freedom

::: {.key-equation data-name="Grübler–Kutzbach formula"}
$$
\text{dof} = m\,(N - 1 - J) + \sum_{i=1}^{J} f_i
$$ {#eq-grubler}
:::

**In words.** Start with the freedom every moving link would have on its own, then take away what each joint forbids.

**Term by term.**

- $m$: freedoms of one rigid body, 3 in the plane and 6 in space.
- $N$: number of links, counting the fixed base (which has no freedom, hence $N - 1$).
- $J$: number of joints; joint $i$ allows $f_i$ freedoms (1 for revolute or prismatic, 3 for spherical) and so removes $m - f_i$.
- The lecture writes it as $m(N - 1) - \sum_i c_i$ with $c_i = m - f_i$ constraints per joint, which is the same thing.

**Quick check.** The two-joint arm on this page: $m = 3$, $N = 3$ (base and two links), $J = 2$, $f_i = 1$, so $\text{dof} = 3 \cdot 0 + 2 = 2$. A 7-joint arm such as the Franka: $m = 6$, $N = 8$, $J = 7$, so $\text{dof} = 0 + 7 = 7$, one more than a 6-number hand pose needs.

### Kinematics

::: {.key-equation data-name="Forward and inverse kinematics"}
$$
x = f(q), \qquad q \in f^{-1}(x) = \{\, q : f(q) = x \,\}
$$ {#eq-kinematics}
:::

**In words.** Forward kinematics is a function from joint angles to hand pose. Its inverse is a *set*: all joint configurations that put the hand at $x$, which may hold several, infinitely many, or no elements.

**Term by term.**

- $q$: the joint configuration, a point in configuration space.
- $x$: the hand's pose (here just its position in the plane).
- $f$: deterministic but not one-to-one, so it has no ordinary inverse.

::: {.key-equation data-name="Inverse kinematics by gradient descent"}
$$
\begin{aligned}
L(q) &= \tfrac12 \lVert f(q) - x^{\text{target}} \rVert^2 \\
q &\leftarrow q - \alpha\, J(q)^\top \big(f(q) - x^{\text{target}}\big)
\end{aligned}
$$ {#eq-ik-step}
:::

**In words.** Measure how far the hand is from the target, and move every joint a little in the direction that shrinks that distance fastest.

**Term by term.**

- $J(q) = \partial f / \partial q$: the Jacobian. Column $j$ is the hand's velocity per unit speed of joint $j$.
- $f(q) - x^{\text{target}}$: the hand's error vector $e$.
- $J^\top e$: the gradient of $L$ by the chain rule; each joint gets the share of the error it can fix.
- $\alpha$: the step size. Too large and the joints overshoot; too small and convergence is slow.

**Why it holds.** $L = \frac12 e^\top e$ with $e = f(q) - x^{\text{target}}$, so $\partial L / \partial q = (\partial e / \partial q)^\top e = J^\top e$. Descent along $-J^\top e$ lowers $L$ for a small enough $\alpha$. It finds *a* solution, the one in whose basin it starts.

**Quick check.** For the target $(0.3, 1.4)$ the exact solutions are $(q_1, q_2) =$ (45\.2°, 75\.2°) and (110\.6°, \-75\.2°). Gradient descent with $\alpha = 0.3$ started from (0°, 57°) reaches the first, within 1 mm after 54 steps; started from (86°, \-57°) it reaches the second, after 78 steps.

::: {#cell-fig-ik .cell execution_count=5}

