**What to try.** Start at the defaults, the start configuration of @fig-cspace. Raise the shoulder angle with the elbow fixed at 30°: at a shoulder angle of 20° the forearm reaches the disc and the dot enters the grey region. Now set the shoulder to 40° and move the elbow through its whole range: the arm touches the disc at every elbow angle, because the upper arm alone already passes through it. At a shoulder angle of 70°, only bending the elbow downwards (negative angles) brings the forearm into the disc. Tick both overlays: the straight line cuts through the grey region, the planned detour stays in white cells, and where the detour reaches an edge of the square it continues on the opposite edge, because the angles wrap around.

### PID tuner


Set the gains, the control frequency, the waypoint scaling, and whether gravity acts. The simulation is the same as the toy's: a 1 kg, 0.5 m rod lifted from -30° to 60° in 1 s, then held, with the physics stepped every millisecond and the controller updating at the chosen frequency.

```{ojs}
pid = FileAttachment("data/w02-pid.json").json()
viewof Kp = Inputs.range([0, 80], {step: 1, value: 30, label: "Kp"})
viewof Ki = Inputs.range([0, 150], {step: 5, value: 60, label: "Ki"})
viewof Kd = Inputs.range([0, 6], {step: 0.25, value: 2, label: "Kd"})
viewof freq = Inputs.select([100, 50, 30, 25, 20, 15, 10], {value: 100, label: "control Hz"})
viewof scaling = Inputs.radio(["quintic", "LERP"], {value: "quintic", label: "waypoints"})
viewof gravityOn = Inputs.toggle({label: "gravity", value: true})
```

```{ojs}
// The same discrete PID and physics as rlc.toys.w02_control.track().
sim = {
  const nSub = Math.round(1 / (freq * pid.dt)), dtc = nSub * pid.dt, n = Math.round(pid.total / pid.dt);
  const f = s => scaling === "LERP" ? s : 10 * s ** 3 - 15 * s ** 4 + 6 * s ** 5;
  const ref = t => pid.q0 + (pid.q1 - pid.q0) * f(Math.min(1, Math.max(0, t / pid.moveT)));
  let q = pid.q0, qd = 0, integ = 0, ePrev = 0, u = 0;
  const out = [];
  for (let k = 0; k < n; k++) {
    const t = k * pid.dt;
    if (k % nSub === 0) {
      const e = ref(t) - q;
      integ += e * dtc;
      u = Kp * e + Ki * integ + Kd * (e - ePrev) / dtc;
      ePrev = e;
    }
    const grav = gravityOn ? -pid.mass * pid.g * (pid.length / 2) * Math.cos(q) : 0;
    qd += (u - pid.friction * qd + grav) / pid.inertia * pid.dt;
    q += qd * pid.dt;
    if (!isFinite(q) || Math.abs(q) > 1e3) return {rows: out, unstable: true};
    if (k % 5 === 0) out.push({t, ref: ref(t) * 180 / Math.PI, q: q * 180 / Math.PI, u});
  }
  const tail = out.filter(d => d.t >= pid.total - pid.hold).map(d => d.ref - d.q);
  return {rows: out, unstable: false, mean: d3.mean(tail), swing: d3.max(tail) - d3.min(tail)};
}
```

```{ojs}
//| label: fig-pid-tuner
//| fig-cap: "Reference (dashed) and actual joint angle (top) and motor torque (bottom) for the chosen gains, control frequency and waypoint scaling."
//| fig-alt: "Interactive figure with sliders for Kp, Ki and Kd, a menu for the control frequency, a choice of quintic or LERP waypoints, and a gravity toggle. The top chart shows the joint angle in degrees over 2 seconds as a solid line against a dashed reference rising from -30 to 60 degrees; the bottom chart shows the motor torque. With the defaults the angle follows the reference closely; with Kd at zero it oscillates; at 20 Hz or below it swings wildly or diverges."
{
  const w = Math.min(width, 640), rows = sim.rows.map(d => ({...d, u: Math.max(-20, Math.min(20, d.u))}));
  const top = Plot.plot({
    width: w, height: 220, marginLeft: 48, style: {background: pid.style.surface},
    x: {domain: [0, pid.total], label: null}, y: {domain: [-60, 100], label: "angle (deg)", grid: true},
    marks: [
      Plot.line(rows, {x: "t", y: "ref", stroke: pid.style.muted, strokeDasharray: "5,3"}),
      Plot.line(rows, {x: "t", y: d => Math.max(-60, Math.min(100, d.q)), stroke: pid.style.blue, strokeWidth: 2})
    ]
  });
  const bottom = Plot.plot({
    width: w, height: 160, marginLeft: 48, style: {background: pid.style.surface},
    x: {domain: [0, pid.total], label: "time (s)"}, y: {domain: [-20, 20], label: "torque (N m)", grid: true},
    marks: [Plot.ruleY([0], {stroke: pid.style.ink}), Plot.line(rows, {x: "t", y: "u", stroke: pid.style.orange, strokeWidth: 1.5})]
  });
  return html`<div>${top}${bottom}</div>`;
}
```

```{ojs}
md`${sim.unstable ? "**Unstable:** the joint diverged." : `Over the last ${pid.hold} s: mean error **${sim.mean.toFixed(2)}°**, swing **${sim.swing.toFixed(2)}°**.`}`
```

**What to try.** The defaults are the toy's PID gains; the mean error over the hold is essentially zero. Set $K_i$ to 0: the arm settles 2\.5° low, the gravity sag of @eq-pid. Then set $K_d$ to 0 as well: the arm swings for the whole hold, because nothing damps the spring. Restore the defaults and lower the control frequency: at 25 Hz the tracking is still fine, at 20 Hz the damper acts too late and the joint swings wildly, and at 10 Hz it diverges. Finally switch to LERP and watch the torque jump at 0 s and 1 s; turn gravity off to see that the integral term is no longer needed.

