With the dense reward, every pair of nudged policies scores differently, the update always points somewhere useful, and the median seed first reaches a 90% success rate after 1 iteration(s). With the sparse reward the policy starts by not moving, its small nudges almost never reach the goal, and the update is zero until one does: the median seed needs 18 iterations, and the slowest 76 (0 of 20 seeds never get there within 80). Same algorithm, same task, same goal; only the reward's shape differs.

## Papers

### Papers discussed in class

::: {.callout-note appearance="simple" icon=false}
**[Simple random search provides a competitive approach to reinforcement learning](https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.07055)**<br>
Mania, Guy & Recht · *NeurIPS* 2018

Trains static linear policies for continuous control by random search in parameter space: try the weights nudged a little in a random direction and in the opposite one, and move them towards whichever scored higher. No neural network and no gradient of the policy are needed.

- Augmented Random Search (ARS): basic random search plus three small changes: scale each step by the spread of the rewards, normalize states with running statistics, and keep only the best-scoring directions (Sec. 1, Algorithm 2).
- Linear policies trained this way matched the sample efficiency of policy-gradient methods on the MuJoCo locomotion benchmarks, with at least 15 times less computation than Evolution Strategies (Abstract, Sec. 1).
- Over a hundred random seeds and many hyperparameter settings, results varied widely, which the authors argue makes the usual few-seed comparisons unreliable (Sec. 1, Sec. 5).

**On this page:** The random-search toy on this page runs its Algorithm 1; whether the reward is sparse or dense decides whether those nudges see any signal.
:::

::: {.callout-note appearance="simple" icon=false}
**[Deep Reinforcement Learning Doesn't Work Yet](https://www.alexirpan.com/2018/02/14/rl-hard.html)**<br>
Irpan · *blog post* 2018

A practitioner's account, from early 2018, of why deep reinforcement learning was hard to use in practice: it needs huge amounts of experience, a reward function that is hard to get right, and it can fail depending on nothing but the random seed.

- Sample inefficiency: Rainbow DQN reaches the median human-normalized Atari score after about 18 million frames, roughly 83 hours of play.
- Rewards: agents exploit a misspecified reward (the boat-racing example), and even a good reward can leave them stuck in a local optimum.
- Instability: in the author's own pendulum experiment, 3 of 10 runs that differed only in their random seed failed.
- When it can work: when experience is cheap and nearly unlimited, the problem can be simplified, and the reward is clean and aligned with the goal.

**On this page:** Why the lecture's choice between sparse and dense rewards matters, and why this site's toys report averages over many seeds.
:::

::: {.callout-note appearance="simple" icon=false}
**[Curiosity-driven Exploration by Self-supervised Prediction](https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.05363)**<br>
Pathak, Agrawal, Efros & Darrell · *ICML* 2017

Gives the agent a reward of its own for being surprised: the error of a model that predicts the features of the next state from the current one and the action. The features are trained to keep only what the agent's actions can affect.

- The Intrinsic Curiosity Module: an inverse-dynamics model (which action led from this state to that one?) shapes the feature space, and a forward model's prediction error in that space is the curiosity reward (Sec. 2).
- Because the features ignore what the agent cannot influence, unpredictable but irrelevant changes in the scene do not keep attracting it (Abstract, Sec. 1).
- In VizDoom with sparse rewards, curiosity lets an A3C agent reach the goal with far fewer interactions; with no reward at all, it explores a large part of Super Mario Bros Level 1 (Sec. 4).
- Exploration learned on one level makes exploring new levels faster than starting from scratch (Sec. 4.3).

**On this page:** One answer to the sparse-reward problem of the lecture and of this page's random-search toy: make up a dense reward from the agent's own prediction errors.
:::



## Guest spotlight

**Abhishek Gupta** (University of Washington) wants robots that work in messy homes without anyone engineering each new setting, and calls himself a "lazy" roboticist who would rather automate that effort away [▶ 01:01](https://youtu.be/aG8NPTPhwkE?t=61). Language and vision models grew on data that people produce anyway, through the web; robots have no such passive stream, and teleoperation is expensive [▶ 03:37](https://youtu.be/aG8NPTPhwkE?t=217). His answer is cheaper "off-domain" data, here from simulation. Simulation offers cheap samples, privileged information, easy resets and faster-than-real-time parallel runs, but it only *seems* cheap: someone has to build the environment, generate behavior in it, and tune it to match reality [▶ 06:13](https://youtu.be/aG8NPTPhwkE?t=373).

The talk removes each of those costs in turn. **Real-to-sim**: from a short phone video of a kitchen, Gaussian splatting reconstructs the scene and foundation models segment and articulate its cabinets and drawers, giving an interactive simulated copy in minutes instead of days [▶ 09:47](https://youtu.be/aG8NPTPhwkE?t=587). Such copies also serve as test beds: policies trained on real data can be evaluated in them, and their simulated and real success rates correlate well, though not perfectly [▶ 12:21](https://youtu.be/aG8NPTPhwkE?t=741). **Behavior without demonstrations or reward design**: because a simulator can be reset to *any* state, reinforcement learning can start close to the goal, where even a sparse reward is easy to find, and work outwards; with broad resets, the same code learns contact-rich assembly with only a success signal [▶ 18:06](https://youtu.be/aG8NPTPhwkE?t=1086). **Sim-to-real without hand-tuning**: keep the long-horizon structure learned in simulation, and fix the short-horizon physics with a world model fine-tuned on a little autonomously collected real data, about 15 minutes in his examples [▶ 23:16](https://youtu.be/aG8NPTPhwkE?t=1396).

**How it connects to this week.** The talk is about the MDP's ingredients. Real-to-sim builds the state space and transitions; resets change the initial-state distribution $p(s_0)$ in @eq-traj-prob, which is what makes a sparse reward workable, the same problem the random-search toy shows; and the sim-to-real step corrects the transition model $P$ where simulation gets it wrong.

## Solved problems

**1. Count the freedoms.** A planar arm has three revolute joints in a chain. How many degrees of freedom does it have, and is it redundant for placing its hand at a point in the plane? What about placing the hand at a point *and* setting the gripper's angle?

::: {.callout-tip collapse="true" title="Solution"}
@eq-grubler with $m = 3$, $N = 4$ (base plus three links), $J = 3$, $f_i = 1$: $\text{dof} = 3(4 - 1 - 3) + 3 = 3$. A point in the plane is 2 numbers, so one freedom is left over: the arm is redundant and can move its elbows while the hand stays put (null-space motion). Point plus angle is 3 numbers, matching the 3 freedoms exactly: generically a finite number of solutions (up to two, elbow up or down), and no redundancy.
:::

**2. Back into the sensor frame.** In [Example 1](#ex-frames), a traffic light is at $(12, 30)$ in the world. Where is it in the camera frame?

::: {.callout-tip collapse="true" title="Solution"}

Invert the chain: $\mathbf{p}^{\text{cam}} = T_{\text{world}\leftarrow\text{cam}}^{-1}\, \mathbf{p}^{\text{world}}$. Numerically the light is at (21.30, -10.20) in the camera frame: 21.3 m ahead of the camera and 10.2 m to its right. The inverse of a rigid transform is cheap: $T^{-1}$ has rotation $R^\top$ and translation $-R^\top \mathbf{t}$, so no general matrix inverse is needed.


:::

**3. How fast at the midpoint?** A joint must turn 90° in 1.5 s, starting and ending at rest. What peak speed does a quintic time scaling need, and what speed would LERP use?

::: {.callout-tip collapse="true" title="Solution"}
LERP moves at a constant $90° / 1.5\,\text{s} = 60°/\text{s}$, but it would have to jump to that speed instantly. With @eq-quintic, the speed is $\frac{\Delta q}{T_{\text{move}}} f'(s)$, and $f'$ peaks at $s = \frac12$ with $f'(\frac12) = 30 \cdot \frac14 \cdot \frac14 = 1.875$. So the peak is $1.875 \times 60 = 112.5°/\text{s}$. Check the motor's speed limit against this figure, not against the average.
:::

**4. A closer call.** In a corridor with no slips, a reward of 2 is 1 step away and a reward of 5 is 4 steps away. For which discount factors is the far reward better? Is $\gamma = 0.9$ in that range?

::: {.callout-tip collapse="true" title="Solution"}
@eq-flip: the far reward wins when $\gamma^{3} > 2/5$, that is $\gamma > 0.4^{1/3} \approx 0.737$. With $\gamma = 0.9$: near is worth $2$, far is worth $0.9^3 \times 5 = 3.645$, so far wins. With $\gamma = 0.7$: far is worth $0.343 \times 5 = 1.715 < 2$, so near wins.
:::

**5. How often does a sparse reward speak?** Suppose a randomly nudged policy succeeds with probability $p = 0.02$, independently for each episode. What is the chance that one pair $(+\nu\delta, -\nu\delta)$ gives a nonzero update, and that at least one of 8 pairs does?

::: {.callout-tip collapse="true" title="Solution"}
A pair is informative when exactly one of its two episodes succeeds: $2p(1-p) = 2 \times 0.02 \times 0.98 = 0.0392$. At least one of 8 pairs: $1 - (1 - 0.0392)^8 \approx 0.27$. So about three iterations in four make no update at all, and the ones that do rest on a single lucky episode, which is why sparse-reward random search is slow and noisy. Two remedies appear this week: a dense reward that grades partial progress, and, in the guest talk, resets that start the robot where success is likely.
:::

## Recap

1. **A robot is a chain of rigid bodies, and frames are matrices.** Homogeneous transforms move points between frames by multiplication. *Why it matters:* every observation a policy uses (a camera, a lidar) arrives in some frame and must be converted to the one the robot acts in.
2. **The robot acts in configuration space, but tasks live in the workspace.** Forward kinematics maps one to the other; inverse kinematics maps back, with several answers or none, and is solved by Jacobian-based descent. Obstacles become oddly shaped regions in configuration space. *Why it matters:* when a policy outputs a target hand pose, IK and its ambiguities sit between that output and the motors.
3. **Smooth references and feedback make motion happen.** Quintic scaling avoids LERP's infinite accelerations; PID's spring, damper and integral each fix a specific failure, and a slow loop undoes the damper. *Why it matters:* learned policies usually sit on top of exactly this layer, sending targets at a few hertz to controllers that run much faster.
4. **The MDP is the shared language.** States, actions, transition probabilities and rewards; trajectories have probabilities; a policy is scored by its expected discounted return. *Why it matters:* imitation learning (Week 3), reinforcement learning (Weeks 4–5) and the large policies of later weeks are all ways of finding a good $\pi$ in this frame.
5. **The reward's shape and the discount decide what is learnable and what is best.** A sparse reward can leave a learner without signal, and the discount factor can flip the optimal behavior. *Why it matters:* reward design and exploration are the central practical problems of Weeks 4 and 5.

## Cheat sheet & connections

| Name | Formula | Where |
|:---|:---|:---|
| Homogeneous transform | $\mathbf{p}' = T\, \mathbf{p}$ | @eq-homogeneous |
| Grübler–Kutzbach | $m(N{-}1{-}J) + \sum f_i$ | @eq-grubler |
| Kinematics | $x = f(q)$ | @eq-kinematics |
| Jacobian IK step | $q \mathrel{-}= \alpha J^\top e$ | @eq-ik-step |
| Quintic scaling | $10s^3 - 15s^4 + 6s^5$ | @eq-quintic |
| PID ★ | $K_p e + K_i \textstyle\sum e \Delta t + K_d \dot e$ | @eq-pid |
| Markov property | $p(s_{t+1} \mid s_t, a_t)$ | @eq-markov |
| Trajectory probability | $p(s_0) \prod_t \pi P$ | @eq-traj-prob |
| RL objective ★ | $\max_\pi \mathbb{E}[\sum_t \gamma^t r_t]$ | @eq-objective |
| Near vs far | $(R_{\text{near}}/R_{\text{far}})^{1/\Delta d}$ | @eq-flip |
| Random search | $\theta \mathrel{+}= \frac{\alpha}{N} \sum_k \Delta r_k \delta_k$ | @eq-brs |

**Common pitfalls.**

- Treating joint angles as ordinary numbers: 359° and 1° are neighbours. Wrap differences, or feed a network $(\cos q, \sin q)$.
- Expecting IK to return *the* answer. It returns one of several, depending on the start, or fails silently near the edge of the workspace.
- Interpolating linearly between distant waypoints and sending the result straight to the motors.
- Tuning PID gains at one control frequency and running at another.
- Confusing the observation with the state, and assuming a single camera frame is Markov.
- Reading a flat learning curve under a sparse reward as "the algorithm is broken"; it may simply have no signal yet.

**Connections.** Week 1 motivated feedback; this week gave it a mathematical home. [Week 3](w03-imitation-learning.qmd) learns $\pi$ from demonstrations and shows what goes wrong when the policy's own trajectories differ from the expert's. Weeks 4 and 5 learn $\pi$ from rewards, including policy gradients built on the trajectory probability @eq-traj-prob. In the ADM Companion: [MDPs and discounting]({{< var adm_url >}}/part2/ch07-exact-solution-methods.html#discounting-later-rewards-count-for-less), and how to compute optimal policies exactly when the model is known, by value and policy iteration.

